{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"NAD(P) binding occurs in several structural and evolutionary lineages, so it is not evidence for a single ancestral NAD(P)-binding enzyme. Unrelated lineages can independently evolve similar cofactor-binding geometries through convergent evolution, constrained by the same cofactor chemistry. Within a homologous lineage, divergent evolution can preserve cofactor binding while changing substrate specificity and reaction chemistry. Multiple folds support multiple structural solutions, but fold differences alone do not prove independent origins or exclude very remote ancestry.

In SCOPe, families group close relatives; superfamilies express probable common ancestry, including distant relatives. Sharing a fold establishes a similar structural framework without necessarily establishing homology, while sharing a class establishes only broad secondary-structure composition. The classic NAD(P)-binding Rossmann fold (c.2) is therefore not a universal homology group for NAD(P)-binding enzymes.

Cofactor contacts, short binding motifs, and catalytic-residue arrangements can arise independently under chemical constraints. Homology requires broader evidence such as statistically convincing sequence/profile similarity, conserved structural topology and residue context, and coherent evolutionary relationships. Cofactor binding or local active-site similarity alone cannot justify transferring substrate specificity, catalytic mechanism, or NAD versus NADP preference, or selecting a whole-domain modeling template or evolutionary representative.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
